{"hash":"4b2658b972421f7e","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":98.557,"said":"And now, here you are, before Shri Somnath. Look around you: this inner shrine, where Somnath and Maa Parvati are worshipped, has been clad in gold by the temple trust, its walls shining in the lamplight. Think of everything that has brought you to this moment: the fading Moon who prayed on this shore, the temples of gold and stone, the raids, the long wait, and the promise made among the ruins. Through all of it people kept coming, just as you have come today, each carrying something to lay down: a worry, a hope, a prayer for someone far away. Take a breath and fold your hands. In the words of the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya: ‘Saurashtradeshe vishade’tiramye jyotirmayam chandrakalavatamsam, bhaktipradanaya kripavatirnam tam Somanatham sharanam prapadye.’ ‘In the bright and lovely land of Saurashtra, radiant, wearing the crescent moon as his crown, who came down in compassion to grant devotion: in that Somanatha I take refuge.’ You have begun where tradition begins: Somnath is the first of the twelve. Pilgrims may visit the others in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Now the drums begin, the conch sounds and the lamps are raised. Let the aarti begin. Har Har Mahadev.\n\nDid you know? Mobile phones and cameras are not allowed inside the temple, so this darshan is one every pilgrim carries home only in memory.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1329},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1345,"end":1471}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,3],[0.314,0.349,4,7],[0.915,0.288,9,13],[1.216,0.168,14,17],[1.396,0.216,18,21],[1.877,0.349,23,29],[2.237,0.276,30,34],[2.526,0.589,35,42],[4.062,0.228,44,48],[4.303,0.385,49,55],[4.699,0.264,56,59],[5.192,0.264,61,65],[5.469,0.204,66,71],[5.685,0.481,72,78],[6.394,0.18,80,85],[6.587,0.589,86,93],[7.188,0